nical Engineer to bring hands-on structural design experience and sound engineering judgement to the mechanical development and verification of Nyx. With a strong system-level mindset, you will support subsystem teams in developing robust designs, resolving mechanical challenges, and substantiating the integrity of critical hardware throughout the development lifecycle.</p><p style="min-height:1.5em"></p><p style="min-height:1.5em">A key focus of your role will be to develop and consolidate the mechanical justifications supporting NASA requirements compliance and Safety Review Panel (SRP) activities. Working with subsystem owners and the safety and certification teams, you will establish the technical basis for allowable damage and failure criteria across critical vehicle components. You will combine direct technical contributions with practical guidance to engineers and suppliers, connecting component-level assessments with vehicle-level requirements and safety considerations.</p><p style="min-height:1.5em"></p><p style="min-height:1.5em">You will be part of the Nyx Design Office Team, within the Cross-Functional Mechanical team, and will report to the Integrated Mechanical Sizing &amp; Assurance Team Leader.</p><h2><br />Key Responsibilities:<br /></h2><h3>Technical Leadership &amp; Integration</h3><ul style="min-height:1.5em"><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Provide hands-on structural design expertise and technical guidance across subsystems, supporting mechanical integrity and consistency at vehicle level.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Assess load paths, interfaces, stiffness, and boundary conditions to understand how mechanical, pressure, and thermo-mechanical loads are transferred through components, attachments, and the vehicle structure.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Perform and review mechanical assessments using engineering calculations, finite element analysis (FEA), and test evidence, challenging assumptions and identifying the failure modes that drive design decisions.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Support subsystem owners in translating high-level mechanical requirements into subsystem and component specifications, maintaining traceability and accounting for vehicle-level interactions.</p></li></ul><p style="min-height:1.5em"></p><h3>Compliance &amp; Verification</h3><ul style="min-height:1.5em"><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Develop and document mechanical justifications for allowable damage and failure criteria for critical Nyx hardware, in collaboration with the relevant subsystem and discipline specialists.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Establish the supporting rationale through analysis and test evidence, addressing relevant strength, stability, fatigue, fracture, damage tolerance, and residual performance considerations as applicable to each component.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Support NASA SRP activities by preparing mechanical justification, contributing to compliance matrices and verification evidence, and addressing technical questions and review actions with the safety and certification teams.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Review qualification strategies, test plans, and reports for consistency with applicable NASA requirements, agreed standards, and the assumptions used in mechanical analyses.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Participate in design reviews, identify gaps in mechanical justification, and recommend design changes or additional verification activities to resolve them.</p></li></ul><p style="min-height:1.5em"></p><h3>Supplier Interactions and Team Collaboration</h3><ul style="min-height:1.5em"><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Review supplier designs, structural analyses, and verification documentation to assess technical maturity and consistency with subsystem and vehicle requirements at key milestones.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Provide practical technical guidance to suppliers and subsystem owners, resolving mechanical issues and following technical actions through to closure.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Facilitate technical alignment between subsystem teams, structural analysis, materials and processes, testing, safety and certification, and suppliers, ensuring that assumptions, interfaces, and responsibilities are clearly understood.</p></li></ul><h2>What We Would Love to See from You</h2><p style="min-height:1.5em"></p><h3> Qualifications:</h3><ul style="min-height:1.5em"><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Bachelor’s or Master’s degree in Mechanical Engineering, Aerospace Engineering, or a related field, or equivalent relevant practical experience.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Minimum 5 years of relevant professional experience in mechanical or structural engineering within the aerospace or aeronautics industry.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Demonstrated hands-on experience designing and justifying structural or mechanical hardware through development and qualification, in aerospace or aeronautics.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Experience assessing relevant failure mechanisms and developing defensible mechanical justifications, including damage acceptance or failure criteria for safety-critical hardware.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Ability to perform and critically review engineering calculations and FEA, interpret CAD models and engineering drawings, and relate analysis assumptions to the physical design and test configuration.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Understanding of mechanical requirements flow-down, qualification and verification processes, and traceable compliance evidence, together with familiarity with applicable space standards and NASA requirements.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Demonstrated ability to guide engineers and suppliers through complex mechanical problems while considering subsystem interfaces and vehicle-level consequences.</p></li></ul><p style="min-height:1.5em"><strong>Additional experience we would value:</strong></p><ul style="min-height:1.5em"><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Direct involvement in NASA safety reviews or SRP submissions, particularly the preparation and defence of mechanical qualification and the closure of review actions.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Experience with pressurized structures, fluid piping, radiators, or propulsion hardware, and with fracture control, damage tolerance, or structural test campaigns.</p></li></ul><h3> Skills and Behaviors:</h3><ul style="min-height:1.5em"><li><p style="min-height:1.5em"><strong>System-Level Thinking:</strong> Ability to connect component-level design and failure mechanisms with subsystem interfaces, vehicle performance, and safety objectives.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em"><strong>Engineering Judgement:</strong> Ability to identify governing load paths and failure modes, challenge modelling assumptions, and distinguish between a numerical result and a sound mechanical justification.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em"><strong>Influence &amp; Decision-Making:</strong> Ability to guide technical decisions across teams and suppliers through clear reasoning and evidence, while working within agreed technical responsibilities.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em"><strong>Mentorship:</strong> Ability to share practical design experience and help engineers strengthen their structural understanding and verification practices.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em"><strong>Stakeholder Management:</strong> Ability to work constructively with internal teams, suppliers, and external reviewers, including NASA, in coordination with the safety and certification teams.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em"><strong>Problem-Solving:</strong> Proactive in identifying mechanical risks, prioritizing substantiation gaps, and developing proportionate solutions that protect technical integrity and program objectives.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5e